{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019BaTjUApQojWZ2Jc2pD5av","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is a clear, owner-specified hardening change. Scanner job logs will go through the existing close-out redactor before they stay on disk, and the redactor gains a fail-closed PII pass with an allow-list. Every path from a log into the repository is audited, and docs/proposal.md is updated to match. Only the engineer triage role exists, so it writes the whole plan, and the engineer worker implements it.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: list every commit path for logs and log-derived text, and design the PII patterns, allow-list and placeholders. Decide whether commit-author emails count as PII. Plan the scanner-log redaction hook, the fail-closed behaviour, the table-driven offline tests and the proposal edits.\n- worker engineer: code change in the redactor and scanner run path, with tests and the docs/proposal.md update.\n\n## Open issues\n- The change spans several packages (close-out redactor, scanner runner, close-out commit paths), but no architect role exists. The engineer plan should settle the approach.\n- No product-manager or documentation-planner role exists either. The proposal update is in scope for the worker.\n- The plan must make one policy call, the issue's open question of whether commit-author emails in git log output count as PII. A maintainer may want to confirm that choice.\n- The optional gitleaks pass and the PII pass both fail closed, so a PII false positive could block commits. Allow-list tuning deserves a careful review.\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\n# Loop poll intervals. Unset loops keep their defaults (scanners 4h, triage\n# 15m, workers 5m, reviewers 5m, prwatch 5m); 1m is the minimum. Hand-offs\n# no longer wait for a poll: a close-out wakes the next loop, a freed slot\n# wakes loops with waiting work, and approvals and unblocks are watched every\n# minute (#394, #406). The merger is controller code with no agent or job\n# slot, so it polls every minute to merge green PRs sooner.\nintervals: { merger: 1m }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make lint test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\nCLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\nmkdocs.yml\ninternal:\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\nbudget\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\nco